\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Reverse norm one is the Spin carrier in real dimension three}

\p{Let #{V} be a three-dimensional real vector space and let #{Q} be
nondegenerate. Suppress the canonical generator map in Clifford products.
For #{x\in\Cl^{+}(Q)}, the following are equivalent:

##{x\text{ is the Clifford value of an element of }\Spin(Q),
\qquad \operatorname{rev}(x)x=1.}

The forward implication is the even-unitary condition
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.evenUnitaryGroup.mem_iff_reverse_mul_self_eq_one}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_spinGroup_toUnits}.}

\p{For the converse, the scalar--bivector decomposition in dimension three
writes #{x=a+Z}. Every nonzero bivector is decomposable. If #{Z\ne0}, let
#{P} be its supporting two-plane. The plane contains #{u} with #{Q(u)\ne0}:
otherwise polarization would give #{P\subseteq P^\perp}, impossible because
#{\dim P=2} and nondegeneracy gives #{\dim P^\perp=1}. Since
#{\bigwedge^2P} is one-dimensional, choose #{w\in P} so that
#{Z=\beta_Q(u,w)}. If #{Z=0}, choose any nonisotropic #{u} and take #{w=0}.
Using the unhalved polar form from \ref{fcap-0027},

##{\beta_Q(u,w)=uw-\frac12B_Q(u,w),\qquad
v:=ux=\left(a-\frac12B_Q(u,w)\right)u+Q(u)w.}

Thus #{v} is a vector. Reversal and the norm-one equation give

##{Q(v)1=\operatorname{rev}(v)v
=\operatorname{rev}(x)uux
=Q(u)\operatorname{rev}(x)x=Q(u)1.}

The real scalar map is injective, so #{Q(v)=Q(u)\ne0}. Consequently

##{x=\left(\frac{u}{Q(u)}\right)v,
\qquad Q\left(\frac{u}{Q(u)}\right)Q(v)=1.}

This is a product of two actual nonisotropic vectors, and TauCeti's pair
criterion places it in Spin
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_mem_spinGroup_of_norm_mul_norm_eq_one}.
The proof includes the scalar case #{Z=0}; it uses neither a normalizer
characterization nor a connectedness argument. The general distinction
between reverse norm one and Lipschitz membership remains the one recorded in
\ref{fcap-0028}.}
